      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "First, let's take the square root of both sides of the equation $(x - 5)^2 = 9$. The square root of $(x - 5)^2$ is $|x - 5|$, and the square root of 9 is $\\pm 3$. So, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[|x - 5| = 3\\]\n\nThis absolute value equation gives us two cases to consider:\n\n1. \\(x - 5 = 3\\)\n2. \\(x - 5 = -3\\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\nFor the first case:\n\\[x - 5 = 3\\]\nAdd 5 to both sides:\n\\[x = 3 + 5\\]\n\\[x = 8\\]\n\nFor the second case:\n\\[x - 5 = -3\\]\nAdd 5 to both sides:\n\\[x = -3 + 5\\]\n\\[x = 2\\]\n\nThe roots of the equation are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\). To find the sum of the roots, we add them together:\n\n\\[8 + 2 = 10\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the roots of the equation $(x - 5)^2 = 9$ is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
