    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\), we first recognize that it is a quadratic equation. We can solve it by taking the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 = 9\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides gives us:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = \\pm 3\n\\]\n\nThis results in two separate equations:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 5 = -3\n\\]\n\nWe solve each equation separately:\n\n1. For \\(x - 5 = 3\\):\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3\n\\]\nAdding 5 to both sides:\n\\[\nx = 3 + 5\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 8\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(x - 5 = -3\\):\n\\[\nx - 5 = -3\n\\]\nAdding 5 to both sides:\n\\[\nx = -3 + 5\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 2\n\\]\n\nThe solutions to the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\) are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\). To find the sum of the roots, we add these solutions together:\n\n\\[\n8 + 2 = 10\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the roots is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
